  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper-level ridging continues to be the dominating pattern \nthrough Friday. This pattern will continue to bring warm and dry \nweather through the work week. Today will be the warmest day of \nthe week, with high temperatures reaching 15 to 20 degrees above \nnormal. The ridge flattens out today, increasing westerly flow \naloft. These increased winds mixes down to the surface, bringing \nbreezy to windy conditions. The combination of the unseasonably \nwarm temperatures, winds, and relative humidity dropping to the \nteens result in elevated fire weather conditions for today. \n\nA pattern change is forecasted Saturday through at least Monday. An \ntrough moving down from British Columbia beginning Saturday will \nbring a cooler and wetter pattern. This trough will usher in much \ncolder air into the region, decreasing snow levels. Ensembles have \nbeen hinting at the possibility of snow levels decreasing to \nlower elevations. Timing, how cold it gets, and overall \nprecipitation amounts vary a lot with each model run, so lots of \nuncertainty exists with this next incoming weather system. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFire Weather This Week:\n\nFuels are still curing, which limits the risk for critical fire \nweather conditions today. However, after today, fuels will become \nmore susceptible for fire growth after the prolonged warming and \ndry period this week. With the ridge flattening again Friday, we \nwill have to monitor for elevated fire weather conditions.\n\nNext Incoming Weather System:\n\nThere remains model variability with how cold it gets this weekend \nand how much precipitation falls. About half of the ensemble members \nof both the GFS and European models show snow reaching the lower \nelevations. There's a 30-60% chance for 1\" of snow across lower \nelevations and a 20-50% chance for 3\" of snow. Considerable \nvariations in models runs still leave details uncertain, but we \nwill watch on how this system trends throughout the week. -Wilson",
